WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:08.120
In vertebrate skeletal muscle, a fibre shortens, yet none of its filaments gets

2
00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:10.300
shorter. How can that be?

3
00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.800
A fibre is one long cell, packed with chains of repeating units called sarcomeres.

4
00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:20.940
Each sarcomere holds thick filaments of myosin and thin filaments of actin.

5
00:00:21.883 --> 00:00:26.223
The signal starts at the neuromuscular junction, where a motor neuron meets the

6
00:00:26.223 --> 00:00:31.643
fibre. The neuron releases acetylcholine, which opens channels in the fibre's

7
00:00:31.643 --> 00:00:36.923
membrane. The membrane depolarizes, and an action potential travels along it.

8
00:00:37.623 --> 00:00:42.223
The signal runs down the T tubules, which are infoldings of the membrane reaching

9
00:00:42.223 --> 00:00:43.223
deep into the fibre.

10
00:00:44.217 --> 00:00:48.177
Next to each T tubule lies the sarcoplasmic reticulum.

11
00:00:48.957 --> 00:00:51.157
It is the fibre's internal calcium store.

12
00:00:51.957 --> 00:00:56.737
A voltage sensor in the T tubule membrane is linked to the store's calcium release

13
00:00:56.737 --> 00:01:02.017
channel. When the voltage changes, the sensor opens the channel, and calcium floods

14
00:01:02.017 --> 00:01:07.797
out. In vertebrate skeletal muscle, calcium entering from outside the cell is not

15
00:01:07.797 --> 00:01:08.937
required for this step.

16
00:01:09.850 --> 00:01:13.510
Troponin is a complex of three proteins on the thin filament.

17
00:01:14.570 --> 00:01:19.190
Tropomyosin, a strand along the thin filament, covers the sites where myosin binds.

18
00:01:20.330 --> 00:01:21.650
Calcium binds troponin.

19
00:01:22.430 --> 00:01:25.610
Troponin then shifts tropomyosin, and the sites are uncovered.

20
00:01:26.710 --> 00:01:31.390
Myosin heads attach to actin, swing, and pull the thin filament toward the middle of

21
00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:34.830
the sarcomere. These attached heads are called cross-bridges.

22
00:01:35.783 --> 00:01:37.823
This is the sliding filament model.

23
00:01:38.663 --> 00:01:40.023
The filaments do not get shorter.

24
00:01:40.943 --> 00:01:42.323
They slide past each other.

25
00:01:43.243 --> 00:01:47.263
The band, the length of the thick filaments, keeps its width while the sarcomere

26
00:01:47.263 --> 00:01:51.243
shortens. Think of two combs pushed together, tooth into tooth.

27
00:01:52.043 --> 00:01:53.283
Each comb keeps its length.

28
00:01:54.143 --> 00:01:55.323
Only the overlap changes.

29
00:01:56.417 --> 00:01:59.837
In vertebrate skeletal muscle, ATP has two jobs.

30
00:02:00.677 --> 00:02:04.137
ATP binding to a myosin head releases it from actin.

31
00:02:04.997 --> 00:02:08.097
Splitting the ATP re-cocks the head for the next stroke.

32
00:02:08.997 --> 00:02:12.457
Separately, pumps in the store use ATP to move calcium back.

33
00:02:13.297 --> 00:02:18.357
As calcium falls, tropomyosin covers the sites again, and the muscle relaxes.

34
00:02:18.957 --> 00:02:21.537
Without ATP, the heads stay stuck to actin.

35
00:02:22.277 --> 00:02:24.157
That is why muscles stiffen after death.

36
00:02:24.157 --> 00:02:28.597
Keep this rule: filaments slide, and ATP lets the heads go.
